arriving in Seoul after days of delays a  specially chartered plane returning 368  people who'd been living in Wuhan around  half the number of South Korean  nationals trapped there and wanting to  leave some were found to have fevers and  taken to hospital the rest were heading  here to one of two makeshift quarantine  centers south of the capital their  presence has led to protests from people  living nearby and police had to remove  roadblocks the government sent a  minister to reassure protesters that he  was met by angry shouts and eggs being  thrown under occupation and of course  local residents will be worried by the  prospect of patients coming to their  area but the corona virus is transmitted  in two ways by contact or by respiratory  droplets so you need to be within 1 to 2  meters of the patient to be in danger as  the convoy bringing the evacuees  approached other people turned out to  welcome them it's only natural for the  country to bring back our citizens and  treat them  what's more the people coming here have  passed the screening and don't have  symptoms after an exhausting night of  travel and health screenings their  journey is finally at an end  but now starts the quarantine in a place  they'll have to call home for the next  two weeks each person will have their  own room and are allowed to keep their  phones and computers but there'll be no  visitors while their conditions are  monitored to make sure they are free  from the corona virus at least as one of  the evacuees told us through their  social media account they're back on  South Korean soil and what they call the  nightmare of Wuhan is behind them  Rob McBride al Jazeera ass and South  Korea
 

